# ==============================================================================
# SOUND SYSTEM DESIGN & ARCHITECTURE NOTES (SAVED VERBATIM — DO NOT EDIT OR DELETE)
# ==============================================================================
# CORE GOAL:
# 1. Eliminate the need for Audacity by allowing all volume, pitch variation, clip randomization,
#    cooldowns, and 2D/3D spatial blend to be tweaked directly in the Unity Inspector.
# 2. Provide centralized volume & bus control (Master, Music, SFX, UI, Ambience) via Unity AudioMixer.
# 3. Decouple gameplay code from Unity's AudioSource using a ScriptableObject "SoundData / AudioCue"
#    pattern so that swapping to FMOD Studio in the future requires ZERO changes to gameplay scripts.
#
# ARCHITECTURE BLUEPRINT:
# 1. SoundData (ScriptableObject):
#    - AudioClip[] clips (picks random or sequential to prevent fatigue)
#    - Volume: base slider [0..1] + random variation (+/- range)
#    - Pitch: base slider [0.5..2.0] + random variation (+/- range)
#    - SpatialBlend: 0 (2D UI/Music) to 1 (3D in-world positioned SFX)
#    - Cooldown / Retrigger limit: prevents ear-piercing sound stacking (e.g. multiple rapid hits)
#    - MixerGroup: routing to Master, SFX, Music, or UI
#
# 2. AudioManager (MonoBehaviour Singleton, DontDestroyOnLoad):
#    - AudioSource Pool for SFX: pre-warmed list of pooled AudioSources so no GameObjects are spawned/destroyed.
#    - Dedicated Music AudioSources: dual-source setup for smooth crossfading between tracks.
#    - Public API:
#        AudioManager.Instance.Play(SoundData data, Vector3 position = default)
#        AudioManager.Instance.PlayMusic(SoundData musicData, float fadeDuration = 1.0f)
#        AudioManager.Instance.SetMasterVolume(float 0..1)
#        AudioManager.Instance.SetSFXVolume(float 0..1)
#        AudioManager.Instance.SetMusicVolume(float 0..1)
#
# 3. FMOD FUTURE TRANSITION ROADMAP:
#    - Phase 1 (Now): Unity AudioMixer + ScriptableObject SoundData + AudioSource Pooling.
#    - Phase 2 (Bridge): Wrap audio triggers behind an interface `IAudioService` (e.g. `UnityAudioService` vs `FmodAudioService`).
#    - Phase 3 (FMOD Migration):
#        * Install FMOD for Unity package.
#        * Replace internal play calls with `FMODUnity.RuntimeManager.PlayOneShot(eventReference, position)`.
#        * Add EventReference field to SoundData (or replace clips with FMOD events).
#        * Route dynamic music into FMOD Studio parameters (e.g. CombatState, Intensity, Health) with quantized transitions.
#        * Gameplay code (PlayerCombat, EnemyController, etc.) remains 100% untouched.

